⭐ BOOK 2 — “THE WORLD WITH MANY BETWEEN-PLACES”

(Early Childhood CTA Edition — ages 5–7)
CC0 · Public Domain


---

PAGE 1 — A BIGGER WORLD

Circle, Square, and Triangle loved their world.

But one day, they noticed something new.

Far away, they saw:

Other circles.
Other squares.
Other triangles.

All playing in their own little groups.

“All different,” Circle said.

“All busy,” Square said.

“All trying their best,” Triangle said.


---

PAGE 2 — SOME GLOWS, SOME NOT

As they watched, they noticed something strange.

Some groups had a bright Between-Place glow.

Other groups looked fuzzy.

Some glows were big.
Some were small.
Some flickered on and off.

“Why do some groups glow easily,” Circle asked,
“and others look stuck?”


---

PAGE 3 — VISITING A NEIGHBOR

They rolled, stood, and pointed their way to visit another group.

That group talked very fast.

Everyone spoke at once.

No one waited.

The glow tried to form…

…but it couldn’t.

Triangle whispered, “They’re not doing anything wrong.”

Square nodded, “They just haven’t slowed down yet.”


---

PAGE 4 — A DIFFERENT GROUP

Next, they visited another place.

This group was very quiet.

No one wanted to speak first.

Everyone waited too long.

The glow appeared…

…but it was very small.

Circle said, “They’re being careful.”

Triangle said, “But maybe too careful.”


---

PAGE 5 — A GROUP THAT PUSHED

Then they saw a group that pushed and pulled.

One shape tried to decide everything.

Others felt squished.

The glow showed up…

…but it turned dim and wobbly.

Square said softly, “The Between-Place doesn’t like pushing.”


---

PAGE 6 — A IMPORTANT DISCOVERY

Circle, Square, and Triangle sat together.

They noticed something important.

The Between-Place wasn’t magic.

It didn’t appear because someone was best.

It didn’t belong to one shape.

Triangle said, “It only shows up when everyone helps it.”


---

PAGE 7 — HOW GLOWS STAY BRIGHT

They made a little list together:

• Go slow enough to hear
• Share space
• Take turns
• Rest when tired
• Try again when mixed-up

Circle smiled, “That’s the same rules we use.”

Square said, “Rules for everyone.”

Triangle said, “Rules that feel fair.”


---

PAGE 8 — HELPING WITHOUT FIXING

When they visited other groups, they didn’t tell them what to do.

They didn’t say, “You’re wrong.”

They didn’t say, “Do it our way.”

They just stood nearby, used the rules themselves, and let their glow show.

Something amazing happened.

Other groups started to glow too.


---

PAGE 9 — MANY BETWEEN-PLACES

Soon the world looked different.

Not one big glow.

Not one perfect shape.

But many Between-Places, appearing and fading, growing and resting, wherever shapes listened together.

“It’s okay if glows look different,” Circle said.

“They don’t have to match,” Square said.

“They just have to help,” Triangle said.


---

PAGE 10 — WHEN GLOWS DISAPPEAR

Sometimes, a Between-Place disappeared.

Someone got tired.

Someone got loud.

Someone forgot to listen.

And that was okay.

Because everyone knew:

You can always make a new one.


---

PAGE 11 — THE BIG IDEA

Circle, Square, and Triangle learned:

You don’t need one glow for the whole world.

You don’t need someone in charge of all glows.

You don’t need to be perfect.

You just need to:

Listen.
Share gently.
Rest.
Try again.


---

PAGE 12 — ENDING PAGE

THE END — AND ALSO THE BEGINNING.

Every group can make its own Between-Place.

Every playground.
Every classroom.
Every home.

Whenever people listen together, a glow can appear.

And the world becomes a place with room for everyone.


