⭐ BOOK 5 — “THE BRIDGE THAT KEEPS GROWING”

(Early Childhood CTA Edition — ages 6–10)
CC0 · Public Domain


---

PAGE 1 — A LONG TIME LATER

Circle, Square, Triangle, Star, and Line had built many things together.

They built paths. They built games. They built songs. They built quiet places.

And everywhere they went, Between-Places appeared.

Sometimes small. Sometimes wide. Sometimes just enough.


---

PAGE 2 — WHEN SOMETHING BROKE

One day, something went wrong.

A path cracked. A game felt unfair. Someone felt left out.

The glow faded.

Everyone stopped.

Triangle said, “Oh no.”

Square said, “We messed up.”

Circle said, “Let’s not run.”


---

PAGE 3 — NO BLAME

No one yelled. No one pointed. No one said, “Whose fault is this?”

They stood together in the quiet.

Star said softly, “Mistakes happen.”

Line nodded, “They show us where to grow.”


---

PAGE 4 — MAKING A NEW BETWEEN-PLACE

They tried something they already knew.

They slowed down. They shared small pieces. They listened. They rested.

The Between-Place came back — a little different than before.

A little bigger.


---

PAGE 5 — THE BRIDGE

The glow stretched outward, connecting where they had been to where they were now.

Triangle smiled, “It’s like a bridge.”

Square said, “It connects mistakes to learning.”

Circle said, “And it doesn’t disappear when we mess up.”


---

PAGE 6 — BRIDGES GROW FROM TRYING

They noticed something surprising.

Every time they: • fixed something together
• listened after a mistake
• tried again kindly

…the bridge grew longer.

Not because they were perfect.

Because they cared.


---

PAGE 7 — WHEN NEW FRIENDS ARRIVED

New shapes came to play.

They didn’t know the rules yet. They made different choices. They brought new ideas.

The bridge didn’t stop them.

It welcomed them.

Star said, “The bridge grows when new friends arrive.”


---

PAGE 8 — NO ONE OWNS THE BRIDGE

The friends learned something important.

The bridge didn’t belong to: • Circle • Square • Triangle • Star • Line

It wasn’t owned. It wasn’t guarded.

It grew whenever people listened together.


---

PAGE 9 — THE GENTLE SECRET

Triangle whispered, “The bridge grows by itself.”

Square said, “We don’t make it grow.”

Circle said, “We just don’t break it.”


---

PAGE 10 — WHAT THE BRIDGE TEACHES

The bridge showed them:

• Being kind is stronger than being right
• Fixing things matters more than winning
• Listening makes room for everyone
• Mistakes are part of growing

These weren’t rules written down.

They were things they did.


---

PAGE 11 — THE BIG IDEA

The friends learned:

You don’t need someone in charge to be good together.

You don’t need to be perfect to belong.

When people try, and listen, and care…

a bridge will grow.


---

PAGE 12 — ENDING PAGE

THE END — AND ALWAYS CONTINUING.

Every time people: • listen after a mistake
• fix something together
• make space for someone new

…the bridge grows a little more.

Maybe you’re standing on one right now.

⭐ BOOK 6 — "THE FRIEND WHO ASKED FOR HELP"
(Early Childhood CTA Edition — ages 5–8)
CC0 · Public Domain
PAGE 1 — A HEAVY THING
One morning, Square found something heavy.
Too heavy to move alone.
He pushed. He leaned. He tried again.
It wouldn't budge.
Square sat down and thought.

PAGE 2 — A HARD QUESTION
Square wanted to ask for help.
But he felt funny inside.
"What if they're busy?"
"What if it's too much?"
"What if I should do this myself?"
The heavy thing sat there, waiting.

PAGE 3 — ASKING
Square found Circle.
"I found something heavy," Square said.
"I can't move it alone. Would you help me?"
Circle didn't ask why.
Circle didn't ask what it was for.
Circle said, "Sure. Let's go."

PAGE 4 — MOVING IT TOGETHER
They pushed together.
It was still heavy —
but it moved.
Not fast. Not perfectly.
But it moved.
Square said, "Thank you."
Circle said, "That's what friends do."

PAGE 5 — CIRCLE'S TURN
A few days later, Circle was rolling along
when she got stuck in a ditch.
She tried to roll out. She couldn't.
She saw Triangle nearby.
"Hey Triangle — I'm stuck. Can you help?"
Triangle walked over and gave her a nudge.
Circle rolled free.
"Thanks," Circle said.
Triangle said, "Of course."

PAGE 6 — NOBODY KEPT COUNT
Square helped Circle.
Circle helped Triangle.
Triangle helped Star.
Star helped Line.
Line helped Square.
Nobody kept a list.
Nobody said, "You owe me."
Nobody said, "That's one each."
It just went around.

PAGE 7 — WHEN SOMEONE SAID NO
One afternoon, Triangle asked Star for help.
Star said, "I can't right now. I'm resting."
Triangle said, "Okay."
And that was it.
No one was mad.
No one was hurt.
Nothing between them changed.
Star rested.
Triangle asked Line instead.
And everything was fine.

PAGE 8 — WHY IT FELT SAFE
The friends noticed something.
Asking for help felt easy —
not because anyone had to say yes,
but because anyone could say no
and it was still okay.
Circle said, "That's why I'm not scared to ask."
Square said, "Because no one gets in trouble for saying no."
Triangle said, "And no one gets in trouble for asking."

PAGE 9 — THE SHAPE THAT ISN'T ASKED FOR
Sometimes, a friend didn't ask at all.
Star saw Line struggling with something
and just walked over and helped.
No question. No request.
Just a friend who noticed.
Line smiled. "How did you know?"
Star said, "I didn't. I just saw."

PAGE 10 — WHAT HELPING FEELS LIKE
When they helped each other, something happened.
Not the Between-Place glow.
Something quieter.
A warmth.
A steadiness.
A feeling that said:
"You're not alone in this."
It didn't have a name.
It didn't need one.

PAGE 11 — THE BIG IDEA
The friends learned:
Asking for help isn't being weak.
Helping isn't being in charge.
Saying no isn't being mean.
When friends help each other
because they care —
not because they have to —
everything feels lighter.
And it goes around.

PAGE 12 — ENDING PAGE
THE END — AND IT KEEPS GOING AROUND.
Every time someone asks for help,
every time someone shows up,
every time someone says "not today" and it's okay…
the world gets a little easier to live in.
Not because of rules.
Because of friends.

